1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,170
In this video, recorded with SDK 0.1.0,
we'll look at decentralized identifiers on

2
00:00:09,170 --> 00:00:18,339
Stellar: what a DID document contains, and
how to create, resolve, update and

3
00:00:18,339 --> 00:00:19,750
deactivate one.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:29,917
A Soroban Identity DID is did colon
stellar colon, followed by a Stellar

5
00:00:29,917 --> 00:00:30,679
address.

6
00:00:30,679 --> 00:00:39,833
Because the identifier comes from the
address, there's exactly one DID per

7
00:00:39,833 --> 00:00:49,750
wallet, and anyone can work out a wallet's
DID without asking the chain.

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,815
The contract stores a DID document for
each controller.

9
00:00:55,815 --> 00:01:06,153
id is the DID string. controller is the
wallet that owns it, and only that wallet

10
00:01:06,153 --> 00:01:08,091
can change it.

11
00:01:08,091 --> 00:01:18,429
metadata is a map of strings to strings,
for things like a display name or a

12
00:01:18,429 --> 00:01:27,474
website. There are also service endpoints,
for pointing to APIs that act for this

13
00:01:27,474 --> 00:01:28,120
identity.

14
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:36,519
created at and updated at are ledger
timestamps, and active says whether the

15
00:01:36,519 --> 00:01:39,750
DID is still in use.

16
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,696
Let's create a DID for Alice.

17
00:01:45,696 --> 00:01:58,039
Build an IdentityClient from the testnet
config plus the three contract IDs, then

18
00:01:58,039 --> 00:02:06,583
call create DID with Alice's keypair and
some metadata.

19
00:02:06,583 --> 00:02:19,875
The SDK builds the transaction, simulates
it to get the resource footprint and fee,

20
00:02:19,875 --> 00:02:30,318
signs it with Alice's key, submits it, and
waits for confirmation.

21
00:02:30,318 --> 00:02:45,509
Every write in the SDK returns the same
shape: data, here the new DID and the

22
00:02:45,509 --> 00:02:59,750
estimated fee, plus the transaction hash,
so you can link to it in an explorer.

23
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,097
Notice the first check in create did:
controller dot require auth.

24
00:03:08,097 --> 00:03:16,194
Soroban verifies that the controller
actually signed the transaction. You can't

25
00:03:16,194 --> 00:03:23,556
create, update or deactivate a DID for
someone else's wallet.

26
00:03:23,556 --> 00:03:35,333
And if Alice tries to create a second DID,
the contract rejects it, and the SDK

27
00:03:35,333 --> 00:03:39,750
throws an error that says so.

28
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:52,946
resolve DID returns the full document,
with metadata as a plain object and

29
00:03:52,946 --> 00:03:55,933
timestamps as numbers.

30
00:03:55,933 --> 00:04:12,863
For a simple yes or no, for example to
gate a feature, use has active DID. It

31
00:04:12,863 --> 00:04:27,800
returns false both for addresses with no
DID and for deactivated ones, so you don't

32
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:30,788
need a try/catch.

33
00:04:30,788 --> 00:04:39,750
Reads are simulations: they're free and
nothing is signed.

34
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:54,531
To change metadata, call update DID with
the controller's keypair.

35
00:04:54,531 --> 00:05:13,422
One thing to know: update replaces the
whole metadata map rather than merging,

36
00:05:13,422 --> 00:05:30,859
and it can't be empty. Send every key you
want to keep.

37
00:05:30,859 --> 00:05:49,750
Resolve again and you'll see the new
metadata and a fresh updated at.

38
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:04,938
Deactivation is a soft delete. The
document stays on-chain for auditability,

39
00:06:04,938 --> 00:06:11,727
but active flips to false.

40
00:06:11,727 --> 00:06:30,739
Only the contract admin can reactivate a
DID, so treat deactivation as permanent in

41
00:06:30,739 --> 00:06:33,455
your app.

42
00:06:33,455 --> 00:06:49,750
Apps should check has active DID, not just
whether a document exists.

43
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:58,803
A quick word on metadata: it's public.
Anyone can read it, forever.

44
00:06:58,803 --> 00:07:10,541
Put things there you'd put on a business
card: a display name, a website, a country

45
00:07:10,541 --> 00:07:17,877
code if you want to be counted in network
analytics.

46
00:07:17,877 --> 00:07:27,414
Never put personal data like emails, birth
dates or document numbers in metadata.

47
00:07:27,414 --> 00:07:34,750
That's what credentials are for, and
they're the next video.

48
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:45,313
You can now create, resolve, update and
deactivate a DID.

49
00:07:45,313 --> 00:07:56,656
Next, we'll issue, verify and revoke
verifiable credentials, the part that

50
00:07:56,656 --> 00:07:59,750
makes identity useful.
